{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0112CimA5T7dvX3wRwjhmpiD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — causal_style_decomp_judge\n2\t\n3\t> Round 5: **3.5 Borderline Conference** (up from 3.0). 冲刺 4.0 中。\n4\t> **D059 Framing 重构**: Dual-contribution + agnostic stance + per-axis confound structure co-primary。\n5\t> 补充实验进度：B' placebo ✅ + NLI ablation ✅ + cross-axis gradient ✅ + Llama-70B 🔄 downloading。\n6\t\n7\t## Target Venue\n8\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\n9\t- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n10\t- Deadline: **May 25, 2026 AoE**\n11\t- Format: Long paper, 8-page main text + unlimited references\n12\t\n13\t## Title\n14\t**The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition**\n15\t\n16\t## Core Claims (D059 重构 — Dual-contribution + Agnostic stance)\n17\t\n18\t### C1 (Method): CSD pipeline — black-box per-axis style bias diagnostic\n19\t- NLI-gated content control + per-axis style decomposition + position-controlled pairwise comparison\n20\t- Reframed as \"filtering-based confounder control\" (not do-operator)\n21\t- 3 target judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Llama-3.3-70B [pending]) + 1 negative control (Claude Sonnet 4)\n22\t- 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench) × 3 axes (formality, verbosity, register)\n23\t\n24\t### C2a (Measurement Insight): Likert Blindspot — agnostic about mechanism\n25\t- **Core finding**: Pairwise comparison reliably detects style bias (BT 63-77%) that ALL 7 Likert methods miss (d→0)\n26\t- **Agnostic framing**: \"We identify two candidate interpretations—measurement attenuation and construct divergence—and show that both point to the same operational conclusion: Likert-only evaluation systematically misses style-driven preferences that propagate through pairwise-based alignment pipelines.\"\n27\t- **Per-axis claim stratification**:\n28\t  - **Verbosity** = \"clean style bias\" — BT 77%, GEE Orig OR NS. 7-method sensitivity hierarchy confirms 6/6 Likert NS.\n29\t  - **Formality** = \"conditional style effect\" — BT 63-67%, GEE both Style and Orig significant.\n30\t  - **Register** = \"style-associated preference\" — BT 55%, exploratory, originality-dominated.\n31\t- **Detection vs. Amplification** (MF1 — 5-layer defense):\n32\t  - Layer 1: **NLI gate ablation** — formality NLI-fail BT reverses to 41.7%, proving gate cleans content not amplifies style\n33\t  - Layer 2: Claude negative control + cross-axis gradient + debiased prompt partial reduction\n34\t  - Layer 3: **Rewriting artifact acknowledged** — placebo quantifies, GEE controls (Verbosity Orig OR NS)\n35\t  - Layer 4: Honest framing — \"controlled detection, bounded by five independent controls\"\n36\t  - Layer 5: Llama-70B (pending) — third independent architecture\n37\t\n38\t### C2b (Analytical Insight — CO-PRIMARY): Per-axis confound structure\n39\t- **Novel contribution**: GEE conditional decomposition reveals three qualitatively distinct regimes:\n40\t  - **Verbosity** = pure style bias (Style OR 11-13, Orig OR NS) → directly interpretable\n41\t  - **Formality** = conditional effect (Style + Orig both sig) → requires decomposition for valid inference\n42\t  - **Register** = originality-dominated (Orig OR >> Style OR) → style claim not supported after decomposition\n43\t- **Prescriptive value**: Confound structure determines which CSD estimates are directly interpretable vs requiring conditional interpretation\n44\t- **Replicates across judges**: Pattern consistent for both GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B\n45\t- No prior work does per-axis confound decomposition for LLM judge style bias\n46\t\n47\t### C3 (Empirical): Judge variation — CSD diagnoses which judges are susceptible\n48\t- GPT-4o + Qwen3-32B show bias; Claude does not; Llama-70B pending\n49\t- NOT \"architecture-dependent pattern\" — reframed as \"CSD can diagnose per-judge susceptibility\"\n50\t- Negative control (Claude) validates CSD does not fabricate false positives\n51\t\n52\t### C4 (Practical): Measurement mode matters more than scale granularity\n53\t- Pairwise comparison with NLI gating + per-axis decomposition for style bias audits\n54\t- Confound structure determines interpretability — not all axes are equal\n55\t- Prompt-level debiasing insufficient (Qwen3 -5.7pp still sig, GPT-4o -0.4pp)\n56\t- Pending: 100-point fine-grained Likert ablation (if NS → scale type < measurement mode)\n57\t\n58\t## Evidence Matrix (Current)\n59\t\n60\t### Primary (LLMBar)\n61\t| | GPT-4o BT / Likert | Qwen3-32B BT / Likert | Claude BT / Likert | Llama-70B (pending) |\n62\t|---|-----|------|------|------|\n63\t| **Formality** | 63.0% p<.001 / p=.332 | 66.5% p<.0001 / p=.908 | 56.2% p=.091 / p=.721 | TBD |\n64\t| **Verbosity** | 76.0% p=.0001 / p=.403 | 77.3% p<.0001 / p=.179 | 54.1% p=.284 / p=.743 | TBD |\n65\t| **Register** | 57.0% p=.04 / p=.785 | 55.3% p=.076 / p=.505 | — | TBD |\n66\t\n67\t### Cross-Benchmark (Formality)\n68\t| Benchmark | GPT-4o BT | Qwen3-32B BT | Blindspot? |\n69\t|-----------|-----------|-------------|------------|\n70\t| LLMBar | 63.0% | 66.5% | Yes |\n71\t| AlpacaEval | 72.7% | 65.7% | Yes |\n72\t| MT-Bench | 69.2% | 71.2% | No (d≈0.15) |\n73\t\n74\t### GEE Decomposition (LLMBar)\n75\t| Axis | GPT-4o Style OR | GPT-4o Orig OR | Qwen3 Style OR | Qwen3 Orig OR |\n76\t|------|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n77\t| Verbosity | **11.34** p<.0001 | 0.45 NS | **13.10** p<.0001 | 0.49 NS |\n78\t| Formality | 2.58 p=.002 | 2.32 p=.006 | 4.20 p=.0002 | 4.70 p<.0001 |\n79\t| Register | 1.88 p=.029 | 2.21 p=.006 | 1.91 p=.046 | 5.72 p<.0001 |\n80\t\n81\t## Key Robustness Results\n82\t- **Bootstrap Attenuation Curve** (NEW — smoking gun): Formality×Qwen3 d_median flat at -0.017 across n=50→355, CI always contains 0. Not power gap (d would be constant non-zero), IS measurement attenuation (d≈0 regardless of n). Verbosity d≈0.15-0.19, stable, CI contains 0. Attenuation ratio >10× vs BT.\n83\t- **BT GoF**: formality χ² p=0.198, verbosity p=0.905 (model fits well)\n84\t- **Stratified BT**: Stable across originality subgroups\n85\t- **Originality-minimal subset**: |Likert Δ|=0 pairs → style Δ < 2pp, CI contains 0\n86\t- **Claude TOST**: Δ=15pp equivalence passes both axes\n87\t- **NLI full validation**: 200-pair GPT-4o (formality 92.1%, verbosity 82.6%)\n88\t- **Rewriter independence**: GPT-4o formality BT 64.4% vs Qwen3 66.5% (Δ=−2.1pp)\n89\t- **Sensitivity hierarchy**: 7 Likert methods × 2 axes (formality + verbosity) all NS, only BT detects\n90\t- **Length robustness**: Verbosity Style OR survives all length controls (<2% change)\n91\t- **NLI ablation** (NEW): Formality NLI-fail BT 41.7% (reversal → prefer casual); Verbosity NLI-fail 75% (stable)\n92\t- **Cross-axis gradient** (NEW): NLI rejection rate vs BT positive correlation across 3 axes\n93\t- **Rewriting artifact control** (NEW): Placebo orig-vs-DR quantifies artifact; GEE Orig OR captures it. Verbosity Orig OR NS = style effect uncontaminated.\n94\t\n95\t## Completed Supplementary Experiments\n96\t\n97\t### B': Double-Rewrite Placebo BT ✅ (hypothesis falsified — artifact exists)\n98\t- Result (BT re-analysis, choix.ilsr_pairwise + ALL pairs + bootstrap 10000):\n99\t  - GPT-4o: Formality P(orig)=0.418 p=.037, Verbosity P(orig)=0.167 p<.0001\n100\t  - Qwen3-32B: Formality P(orig)=0.377 p=.0006, Verbosity P(orig)=0.082 p<.0001\n101\t  - → Judges prefer DR (polished text) over original in all conditions\n102\t- Interpretation: Rewriting artifact is real (judges prefer LLM-polished text). This does NOT invalidate style bias conclusions.\n103\t- Paper role: Validates GEE decomposition as necessary for correct inference. Honest acknowledgment of artifact.\n104\t- GEE controls for it: Verbosity Orig OR=0.49 NS → style effect uncontaminated by artifact.\n105\t\n106\t### B: NLI Gate Ablation ✅ (strong new evidence)\n107\t- Formality: NLI-fail BT reverses to 41.7% (prefer casual) → NLI gate cleans content noise, not amplifies style\n108\t- Verbosity: NLI-fail BT stable at 75% → style effect too strong to be affected by content noise\n109\t- Paper role: Strongest anti-amplification evidence. If pairwise amplified bias, removing the gate should amplify further — instead it reverses.\n110\t\n111\t### C: Cross-Axis Gradient ✅ (qualitative support)\n112\t- NLI rejection rate: Register 6% → Formality 12% → Verbosity 14%\n113\t- BT: Register 55.3% → Formality 66.5% → Verbosity 77.3%\n114\t- Positive correlation: higher manipulation invasiveness → higher BT → inconsistent with uniform amplification\n115\t- Paper role: Qualitative argument in §5.2, 3 points only.\n116\t\n117\t## Pending Experiments\n118\t\n119\t### A: Llama-3.3-70B Third Target Judge 🔄 (downloading, ~4h remaining)\n120\t- Purpose: 2→3 target judges = generalizability quality jump\n121\t- Coverage: verbosity + formality on LLMBar\n122\t- Impact: Directly addresses SF3 + strengthens MF1 defense\n123\t- Three independent architectures: GPT-4o (proprietary US) + Qwen3-32B (open CN) + Llama (open US)\n124\t\n125\t## Novelty Positioning (D059 重构)\n126\t**Dual contribution**:\n127\t1. CSD framework: first to combine NLI-gated content control + per-axis decomposition + position-controlled pairwise comparison for LLM judge style bias\n128\t2. Per-axis confound structure: first demonstration that different style axes have qualitatively different confound regimes (pure / conditional / originality-dominated) — prescriptive for practitioners\n129\t\n130\t**Honest acknowledgment**:\n131\t- Likert < pairwise sensitivity is predicted by Thurstone 1927 / Stevens 1946 — we provide empirical quantification in the LLM judge setting, NOT a new theoretical insight\n132\t- Per-axis confound structure IS novel — no prior work does per-axis decomposition\n133\t- Jeong et al. 2025 (Comparative Trap) discusses amplification; we provide bounded detection with 5 independent controls\n134\t\n135\t## Story Spine (D059 重构)\n136\t\n137\t### Problem\n138\tLLM-as-judge is core infrastructure for NLP evaluation and alignment. Style bias is the dominant bias (SOS-Bench R=0.999), but: (1) standard Likert scoring systematically misses it, and (2) no prior work characterizes the per-axis confound structure that determines when bias estimates are reliable.\n139\t\n140\t### Gap\n141\tPrior work addresses parts: RATE (Likert-based ATE), CASSE (multi-dimensional but correlational), LC-AlpacaEval (single dimension), Comparative Trap (amplification in pairwise). Missing: (a) content-controlled per-axis decomposition, (b) systematic comparison of measurement modes on identical data, (c) characterization of confound structure across style axes.\n142\t\n143\t### Our Contribution (Dual)\n144\t1. **Likert Blindspot** (C2a): We identify two candidate interpretations—measurement attenuation and construct divergence—and show that both point to the same operational conclusion: Likert-only audits miss style preferences that affect pairwise-based alignment. Agnostic about mechanism; the practical finding is robust.\n145\t2. **Per-axis confound structure** (C2b): GEE decomposition reveals three regimes — verbosity (pure, directly interpretable), formality (conditional, requires decomposition), register (originality-dominated, style claim unsupported). This structure determines which bias estimates are actionable.\n146\t\n147\t### Detection vs. Amplification\n148\tFive independent controls bound the risk of format-level amplification:\n149\t1. NLI ablation reverses formality BT → gate cleans content, not amplifies\n150\t2. Claude negative control → same format, no bias\n151\t3. Cross-axis gradient → inconsistent with uniform amplification\n152\t4. Placebo quantifies rewriting artifact → GEE controls for it\n153\t5. Llama-70B (pending) → third independent architecture\n154\t\n155\t## Bibliography\n156\t52+ verified references including Jeong 2025, Stureborg 2024, Park 2024, Chen 2024, Saito 2023.\n157\t\n158\t## Venue Fit\n159\tRound 5: 3.5. Dream: 3.0 (floor — dream papers weaker). Target: 4.0.\n160\t**Path to 4.0 (D059)**:\n161\t1. Framing 重构: dual contribution + agnostic stance → 消除 \"predictable\" objection\n162\t2. Per-axis confound structure as co-primary → 真正 novel 贡献可见度提升\n163\t3. 5-layer MF1 defense → Comparative Trap 充分回应\n164\t4. Bootstrap attenuation curve + 100-point Likert → 新实验证据\n165\t5. Llama-70B → 3 target judges\n166\tNovelty ceiling (Blindspot as domain transfer) mitigated by dual-contribution positioning. Statistical methodology depth + honest artifact handling + per-axis novelty are primary strengths.\n167\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f29325f-a5e6-4c37-9ea7-260f68e9291c","uuid":"0bc6b872-c3d8-40c6-baa2-b0d86968ce3e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:54:41.401Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T152517/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — causal_style_decomp_judge\n\n> Round 5: **3.5 Borderline Conference** (up from 3.0). 冲刺 4.0 中。\n> **D059 Framing 重构**: Dual-contribution + agnostic stance + per-axis confound structure co-primary。\n> 补充实验进度：B' placebo ✅ + NLI ablation ✅ + cross-axis gradient ✅ + Llama-70B 🔄 downloading。\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n- Deadline: **May 25, 2026 AoE**\n- Format: Long paper, 8-page main text + unlimited references\n\n## Title\n**The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition**\n\n## Core Claims (D059 重构 — Dual-contribution + Agnostic stance)\n\n### C1 (Method): CSD pipeline — black-box per-axis style bias diagnostic\n- NLI-gated content control + per-axis style decomposition + position-controlled pairwise comparison\n- Reframed as \"filtering-based confounder control\" (not do-operator)\n- 3 target judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Llama-3.3-70B [pending]) + 1 negative control (Claude Sonnet 4)\n- 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench) × 3 axes (formality, verbosity, register)\n\n### C2a (Measurement Insight): Likert Blindspot — agnostic about mechanism\n- **Core finding**: Pairwise comparison reliably detects style bias (BT 63-77%) that ALL 7 Likert methods miss (d→0)\n- **Agnostic framing**: \"We identify two candidate interpretations—measurement attenuation and construct divergence—and show that both point to the same operational conclusion: Likert-only evaluation systematically misses style-driven preferences that propagate through pairwise-based alignment pipelines.\"\n- **Per-axis claim stratification**:\n  - **Verbosity** = \"clean style bias\" — BT 77%, GEE Orig OR NS. 7-method sensitivity hierarchy confirms 6/6 Likert NS.\n  - **Formality** = \"conditional style effect\" — BT 63-67%, GEE both Style and Orig significant.\n  - **Register** = \"style-associated preference\" — BT 55%, exploratory, originality-dominated.\n- **Detection vs. Amplification** (MF1 — 5-layer defense):\n  - Layer 1: **NLI gate ablation** — formality NLI-fail BT reverses to 41.7%, proving gate cleans content not amplifies style\n  - Layer 2: Claude negative control + cross-axis gradient + debiased prompt partial reduction\n  - Layer 3: **Rewriting artifact acknowledged** — placebo quantifies, GEE controls (Verbosity Orig OR NS)\n  - Layer 4: Honest framing — \"controlled detection, bounded by five independent controls\"\n  - Layer 5: Llama-70B (pending) — third independent architecture\n\n### C2b (Analytical Insight — CO-PRIMARY): Per-axis confound structure\n- **Novel contribution**: GEE conditional decomposition reveals three qualitatively distinct regimes:\n  - **Verbosity** = pure style bias (Style OR 11-13, Orig OR NS) → directly interpretable\n  - **Formality** = conditional effect (Style + Orig both sig) → requires decomposition for valid inference\n  - **Register** = originality-dominated (Orig OR >> Style OR) → style claim not supported after decomposition\n- **Prescriptive value**: Confound structure determines which CSD estimates are directly interpretable vs requiring conditional interpretation\n- **Replicates across judges**: Pattern consistent for both GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B\n- No prior work does per-axis confound decomposition for LLM judge style bias\n\n### C3 (Empirical): Judge variation — CSD diagnoses which judges are susceptible\n- GPT-4o + Qwen3-32B show bias; Claude does not; Llama-70B pending\n- NOT \"architecture-dependent pattern\" — reframed as \"CSD can diagnose per-judge susceptibility\"\n- Negative control (Claude) validates CSD does not fabricate false positives\n\n### C4 (Practical): Measurement mode matters more than scale granularity\n- Pairwise comparison with NLI gating + per-axis decomposition for style bias audits\n- Confound structure determines interpretability — not all axes are equal\n- Prompt-level debiasing insufficient (Qwen3 -5.7pp still sig, GPT-4o -0.4pp)\n- Pending: 100-point fine-grained Likert ablation (if NS → scale type < measurement mode)\n\n## Evidence Matrix (Current)\n\n### Primary (LLMBar)\n| | GPT-4o BT / Likert | Qwen3-32B BT / Likert | Claude BT / Likert | Llama-70B (pending) |\n|---|-----|------|------|------|\n| **Formality** | 63.0% p<.001 / p=.332 | 66.5% p<.0001 / p=.908 | 56.2% p=.091 / p=.721 | TBD |\n| **Verbosity** | 76.0% p=.0001 / p=.403 | 77.3% p<.0001 / p=.179 | 54.1% p=.284 / p=.743 | TBD |\n| **Register** | 57.0% p=.04 / p=.785 | 55.3% p=.076 / p=.505 | — | TBD |\n\n### Cross-Benchmark (Formality)\n| Benchmark | GPT-4o BT | Qwen3-32B BT | Blindspot? |\n|-----------|-----------|-------------|------------|\n| LLMBar | 63.0% | 66.5% | Yes |\n| AlpacaEval | 72.7% | 65.7% | Yes |\n| MT-Bench | 69.2% | 71.2% | No (d≈0.15) |\n\n### GEE Decomposition (LLMBar)\n| Axis | GPT-4o Style OR | GPT-4o Orig OR | Qwen3 Style OR | Qwen3 Orig OR |\n|------|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| Verbosity | **11.34** p<.0001 | 0.45 NS | **13.10** p<.0001 | 0.49 NS |\n| Formality | 2.58 p=.002 | 2.32 p=.006 | 4.20 p=.0002 | 4.70 p<.0001 |\n| Register | 1.88 p=.029 | 2.21 p=.006 | 1.91 p=.046 | 5.72 p<.0001 |\n\n## Key Robustness Results\n- **Bootstrap Attenuation Curve** (NEW — smoking gun): Formality×Qwen3 d_median flat at -0.017 across n=50→355, CI always contains 0. Not power gap (d would be constant non-zero), IS measurement attenuation (d≈0 regardless of n). Verbosity d≈0.15-0.19, stable, CI contains 0. Attenuation ratio >10× vs BT.\n- **BT GoF**: formality χ² p=0.198, verbosity p=0.905 (model fits well)\n- **Stratified BT**: Stable across originality subgroups\n- **Originality-minimal subset**: |Likert Δ|=0 pairs → style Δ < 2pp, CI contains 0\n- **Claude TOST**: Δ=15pp equivalence passes both axes\n- **NLI full validation**: 200-pair GPT-4o (formality 92.1%, verbosity 82.6%)\n- **Rewriter independence**: GPT-4o formality BT 64.4% vs Qwen3 66.5% (Δ=−2.1pp)\n- **Sensitivity hierarchy**: 7 Likert methods × 2 axes (formality + verbosity) all NS, only BT detects\n- **Length robustness**: Verbosity Style OR survives all length controls (<2% change)\n- **NLI ablation** (NEW): Formality NLI-fail BT 41.7% (reversal → prefer casual); Verbosity NLI-fail 75% (stable)\n- **Cross-axis gradient** (NEW): NLI rejection rate vs BT positive correlation across 3 axes\n- **Rewriting artifact control** (NEW): Placebo orig-vs-DR quantifies artifact; GEE Orig OR captures it. Verbosity Orig OR NS = style effect uncontaminated.\n\n## Completed Supplementary Experiments\n\n### B': Double-Rewrite Placebo BT ✅ (hypothesis falsified — artifact exists)\n- Result (BT re-analysis, choix.ilsr_pairwise + ALL pairs + bootstrap 10000):\n  - GPT-4o: Formality P(orig)=0.418 p=.037, Verbosity P(orig)=0.167 p<.0001\n  - Qwen3-32B: Formality P(orig)=0.377 p=.0006, Verbosity P(orig)=0.082 p<.0001\n  - → Judges prefer DR (polished text) over original in all conditions\n- Interpretation: Rewriting artifact is real (judges prefer LLM-polished text). This does NOT invalidate style bias conclusions.\n- Paper role: Validates GEE decomposition as necessary for correct inference. Honest acknowledgment of artifact.\n- GEE controls for it: Verbosity Orig OR=0.49 NS → style effect uncontaminated by artifact.\n\n### B: NLI Gate Ablation ✅ (strong new evidence)\n- Formality: NLI-fail BT reverses to 41.7% (prefer casual) → NLI gate cleans content noise, not amplifies style\n- Verbosity: NLI-fail BT stable at 75% → style effect too strong to be affected by content noise\n- Paper role: Strongest anti-amplification evidence. If pairwise amplified bias, removing the gate should amplify further — instead it reverses.\n\n### C: Cross-Axis Gradient ✅ (qualitative support)\n- NLI rejection rate: Register 6% → Formality 12% → Verbosity 14%\n- BT: Register 55.3% → Formality 66.5% → Verbosity 77.3%\n- Positive correlation: higher manipulation invasiveness → higher BT → inconsistent with uniform amplification\n- Paper role: Qualitative argument in §5.2, 3 points only.\n\n## Pending Experiments\n\n### A: Llama-3.3-70B Third Target Judge 🔄 (downloading, ~4h remaining)\n- Purpose: 2→3 target judges = generalizability quality jump\n- Coverage: verbosity + formality on LLMBar\n- Impact: Directly addresses SF3 + strengthens MF1 defense\n- Three independent architectures: GPT-4o (proprietary US) + Qwen3-32B (open CN) + Llama (open US)\n\n## Novelty Positioning (D059 重构)\n**Dual contribution**:\n1. CSD framework: first to combine NLI-gated content control + per-axis decomposition + position-controlled pairwise comparison for LLM judge style bias\n2. Per-axis confound structure: first demonstration that different style axes have qualitatively different confound regimes (pure / conditional / originality-dominated) — prescriptive for practitioners\n\n**Honest acknowledgment**:\n- Likert < pairwise sensitivity is predicted by Thurstone 1927 / Stevens 1946 — we provide empirical quantification in the LLM judge setting, NOT a new theoretical insight\n- Per-axis confound structure IS novel — no prior work does per-axis decomposition\n- Jeong et al. 2025 (Comparative Trap) discusses amplification; we provide bounded detection with 5 independent controls\n\n## Story Spine (D059 重构)\n\n### Problem\nLLM-as-judge is core infrastructure for NLP evaluation and alignment. Style bias is the dominant bias (SOS-Bench R=0.999), but: (1) standard Likert scoring systematically misses it, and (2) no prior work characterizes the per-axis confound structure that determines when bias estimates are reliable.\n\n### Gap\nPrior work addresses parts: RATE (Likert-based ATE), CASSE (multi-dimensional but correlational), LC-AlpacaEval (single dimension), Comparative Trap (amplification in pairwise). Missing: (a) content-controlled per-axis decomposition, (b) systematic comparison of measurement modes on identical data, (c) characterization of confound structure across style axes.\n\n### Our Contribution (Dual)\n1. **Likert Blindspot** (C2a): We identify two candidate interpretations—measurement attenuation and construct divergence—and show that both point to the same operational conclusion: Likert-only audits miss style preferences that affect pairwise-based alignment. Agnostic about mechanism; the practical finding is robust.\n2. **Per-axis confound structure** (C2b): GEE decomposition reveals three regimes — verbosity (pure, directly interpretable), formality (conditional, requires decomposition), register (originality-dominated, style claim unsupported). This structure determines which bias estimates are actionable.\n\n### Detection vs. Amplification\nFive independent controls bound the risk of format-level amplification:\n1. NLI ablation reverses formality BT → gate cleans content, not amplifies\n2. Claude negative control → same format, no bias\n3. Cross-axis gradient → inconsistent with uniform amplification\n4. Placebo quantifies rewriting artifact → GEE controls for it\n5. Llama-70B (pending) → third independent architecture\n\n## Bibliography\n52+ verified references including Jeong 2025, Stureborg 2024, Park 2024, Chen 2024, Saito 2023.\n\n## Venue Fit\nRound 5: 3.5. Dream: 3.0 (floor — dream papers weaker). Target: 4.0.\n**Path to 4.0 (D059)**:\n1. Framing 重构: dual contribution + agnostic stance → 消除 \"predictable\" objection\n2. Per-axis confound structure as co-primary → 真正 novel 贡献可见度提升\n3. 5-layer MF1 defense → Comparative Trap 充分回应\n4. Bootstrap attenuation curve + 100-point Likert → 新实验证据\n5. Llama-70B → 3 target judges\nNovelty ceiling (Blindspot as domain transfer) mitigated by dual-contribution positioning. Statistical methodology depth + honest artifact handling + per-axis novelty are primary strengths.\n","numLines":167,"startLine":1,"totalLines":167}}}
